 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a Myrtle Beach home get the 4% rate automatically?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The owner must apply to the county for the 4% rate before the first penalty date. Without the application, the home is taxed at 6%, like a second home."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get the 25% exemption on a second home I buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the home is taxed as a second home or rental and changed hands after 2010. The owner must notify the assessor before Jan. 31 of the first tax year claimed. The taxable value cannot fall below the seller's value on the roll."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a home keep the 4% rate after the owner dies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, for a time, under Act 140. The rate stays until the estate closes, a deed out of the estate is recorded, or Dec. 31 of the year after the death, whichever comes first. It ends if the home is rented more than 72 days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Did Annie Andrews' win change property taxes in Horry County?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The two rates are in the South Carolina Constitution. Only the state legislature, or the legislature and the state's voters together, can change them. A U.S. senator has no vote on them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will my property tax go up at the next Horry County reassessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you have not sold or added on, your taxable value can rise no more than 15% at that reassessment. The tax rate can still change. We expect the next one for tax year 2029, from the state's five-year rule."}}]}</script>
