 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/relocating/jobs/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are there jobs available in Myrtle Beach right now?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and growth is the reason. Employers in Horry County added jobs at nearly twice the national rate between 2019 and 2025, 9.1 percent against 5.1 percent. The Census Bureau ranked this metro second in the country for percent population growth in the year to July 2025. Hiring is steadiest in health care, education, retail, hospitality and the trades."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you find a job in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the field. Health care, education, the trades, retail and hospitality hire steadily here, and the county had 146,055 covered jobs across 12,762 workplaces in 2025. Corporate and senior management roles are scarce because there is no headquarters here. Manufacturing is 2.3 percent of jobs against 8.1 percent nationally."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the average salary in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The average job in Horry County paid $50,053 in 2025, against $78,722 nationally, so about 64 cents on the national dollar. Median household income is $66,880 against $80,734 nationally. The first figure counts jobs and the second counts households, so a two-earner household is better described by the second."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the biggest employers in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the county’s own audited list as of June 2025: Horry County School District at 6,643, Wal-Mart and Sam’s Club at 2,813, Horry County Government at 2,652, Conway Medical Center at 2,035 and McLeod Health at 1,777. The ten largest employ about 12.3 percent of everyone working here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is work in Myrtle Beach seasonal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A quarter of the jobs are in leisure and hospitality, so the county unemployment rate moves on a schedule. It was 7.0 percent in January 2026 and 4.2 percent in April 2026. Salaried work in hospitals, schools and county government does not follow that pattern; hourly hospitality work and visitor-facing trades do."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which jobs pay closest to the national average here?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Education and health services, at $1,244 a week here against $1,278 nationally. Construction and financial activities also pay well by local standards. The widest gaps are in information and professional and business services, where the national average is roughly double the local one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a local job to buy a house in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Your lender qualifies you on your own income, wherever it comes from, and plenty of buyers here keep a remote job at out-of-state pay. If you will be earning locally instead, work out what a local salary supports before you set a price range."}}]}</script>
