 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/airbnb-income/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does an Airbnb make in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The average listing grosses between $25,800 and $42,000 a year, depending on the data vendor and how it counts. Expenses typically consume 55 to 65 percent of gross, so a well-run unit nets roughly $8,000 to $23,000 a year before the mortgage. Top-decile listings gross more than $6,700 a month while the median does about $2,500."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Airbnb profitable in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It can be, but the result usually comes down to how the unit is managed. The same $26,000-gross oceanfront condotel nets roughly $1,600 a year on a 40 percent onsite rental program and roughly $8,000 self-managed. Whole homes in North Myrtle Beach, Surfside Beach, and Murrells Inlet carry the strongest net cash flow."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What occupancy should I expect from a Myrtle Beach Airbnb?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"About 31 to 36 percent of all calendar nights, 53 percent of available nights, or around 63 percent if you run it full-time like the median active host. The measure matters as much as the market."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a license to run an Airbnb in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The City of Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach both require a business license for every rental, with fees based on gross receipts, and unincorporated Horry County requires a county license plus a hospitality-fee account. Guests also pay about 13 percent in combined lodging taxes, most of which Airbnb collects and remits automatically."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which part of the Grand Strand earns the most on Airbnb?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Per listing, Murrells Inlet ($47,603) and Surfside Beach ($42,902) average the most, and Myrtle Beach proper ($25,807) earns less than either, because the city's inventory is two-thirds one- and two-bedroom condos while the south strand is mostly whole homes. Bigger properties gross more; the area difference is a unit-mix effect, not a location premium."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much do Airbnb expenses run in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Plan on 55 to 65 percent of gross for a managed unit. Professional management takes 20 to 30 percent, or 35 to 45 percent on an onsite condotel desk. HOA dues on oceanfront towers are $400 to $1,200 a month. Add the 6 percent property tax assessment and short-term-rental insurance at roughly two to three times a standard homeowner policy. Platform fees come on top; for Airbnb, those are migrating to a 15.5 percent host-only fee in 2026."}}]}</script>
