 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can Myrtle Beach ban short-term rentals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. No South Carolina law stops a city from barring them through zoning. Myrtle Beach bars stays of under 90 days in every zone that begins with R, except RMV. Fewer than 30 houses citywide are grandfathered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Did the 2026 election change short-term rental rules on the Grand Strand?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Horry County, Myrtle Beach, North Myrtle Beach and Surfside Beach had no local ballot question. No state law on the subject passed. The rules change only by a council vote or a new state law."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What was H. 3861?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A 2025 South Carolina House bill that would have barred cities and counties from banning short-term rentals of fewer than 29 days. A government that kept a ban would have lost its state aid. It never got a committee vote."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much tax does a short-term rental pay in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The state charges 7% of the rent. A city or county may add up to 3%. Rentals of 90 days or more to the same guest owe no accommodations tax."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What did Alan Wilson say about short-term rentals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Nothing we found. His priorities page has no short-term rental item. As governor he could sign or veto a bill on the subject."}}]}</script>
